1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,215
This is a whole year of sales from a spreadsheet.

2
00:00:03,215 --> 00:00:06,544
And this dashboard was drawn by twenty lines of Python.

3
00:00:06,544 --> 00:00:09,691
No clicking through chart menus, no dragging ranges.

4
00:00:09,691 --> 00:00:15,682
Change the numbers in the spreadsheet, run the script again, and every chart updates in one second.

5
00:00:16,382 --> 00:00:25,553
In the next ten minutes, you'll go from a plain Excel file to a line chart, a bar chart, a pie chart and a full dashboard, saved as an image you can paste anywhere.

6
00:00:25,553 --> 00:00:27,510
Step by step, no experience needed.

7
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:34,332
And I'll show you the three errors that stop almost every beginner, with the real error messages, and how to fix each one.

8
00:00:35,032 --> 00:00:35,960
Here's the plan.

9
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,932
First, install two free libraries.

10
00:00:37,932 --> 00:00:40,135
Then read the spreadsheet into Python.

11
00:00:40,135 --> 00:00:43,267
Then draw a line chart, and make it look professional.

12
00:00:43,267 --> 00:00:45,180
Then a bar chart and a pie chart.

13
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:49,356
And finally, put everything into one dashboard and save it as a picture.

14
00:00:50,055 --> 00:00:52,779
You might be thinking, Excel already makes charts.

15
00:00:52,779 --> 00:00:53,051
True.

16
00:00:53,051 --> 00:00:58,879
But every month you click through the same menus, select the same ranges, and fix the same titles, by hand.

17
00:00:58,879 --> 00:01:00,948
With Python, you write the steps once.

18
00:01:00,948 --> 00:01:06,612
Next month, you run one command, and every chart is rebuilt, exactly the same way, from the new numbers.

19
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:09,499
And it works the same with ten rows, or ten thousand.

20
00:01:10,199 --> 00:01:11,653
You need Python installed.

21
00:01:11,653 --> 00:01:14,896
If you don't have it yet, get it free from python dot org.

22
00:01:14,896 --> 00:01:18,754
Then open a terminal and type pip install pandas matplotlib openpyxl.

23
00:01:18,754 --> 00:01:20,655
Pandas reads and organizes tables.

24
00:01:20,655 --> 00:01:22,221
Matplotlib draws the charts.

25
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:25,855
And openpyxl is the small helper pandas uses to open Excel files.

26
00:01:25,855 --> 00:01:28,707
To check that everything worked, run this one line.

27
00:01:28,707 --> 00:01:29,993
It prints the versions.

28
00:01:29,993 --> 00:01:32,565
If you see numbers and no error, you're ready.

29
00:01:33,265 --> 00:01:35,822
Here's our practice file, sales dot xlsx.

30
00:01:35,822 --> 00:01:38,816
A small café, twelve months, and three products.

31
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:40,188
Coffee, tea and cakes.

32
00:01:40,188 --> 00:01:42,371
The first row has the column names.

33
00:01:42,371 --> 00:01:47,174
That matters, because pandas uses that first row as the names of the columns.

34
00:01:47,174 --> 00:01:50,105
Put the file in the same folder as your script.

35
00:01:50,805 --> 00:01:51,324
Step one.

36
00:01:51,324 --> 00:01:53,456
Create a file called step one dot py.

37
00:01:53,456 --> 00:01:56,107
Import pandas, and give it the short name p d.

38
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:59,333
Everybody does this, so you'll see it in every tutorial.

39
00:01:59,333 --> 00:02:01,984
Then read excel, with the file name in quotes.

40
00:02:01,984 --> 00:02:05,960
The whole sheet lands in a variable called d f, short for data frame.

41
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,092
That's just pandas' word for a table.

42
00:02:08,092 --> 00:02:08,841
And print it.

43
00:02:09,541 --> 00:02:11,687
Run it with python step one dot py.

44
00:02:11,687 --> 00:02:13,894
On a Mac, it's usually python three.

45
00:02:13,894 --> 00:02:16,592
And there's your spreadsheet, inside Python.

46
00:02:16,592 --> 00:02:17,328
Twelve rows.

47
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:21,865
The numbers on the left are the row numbers pandas adds, starting at zero.

48
00:02:21,865 --> 00:02:23,337
Your data didn't change.

49
00:02:23,337 --> 00:02:24,440
It's just a label.

50
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:26,528
Two quick variations.

51
00:02:26,528 --> 00:02:31,549
If your workbook has several tabs, add sheet name, with the name of the tab.

52
00:02:31,549 --> 00:02:35,051
And if your data is a CSV file, use read csv instead.

53
00:02:35,051 --> 00:02:38,817
Everything else in this video works exactly the same way.

54
00:02:38,817 --> 00:02:43,773
Print d f dot columns any time, to see the exact column names pandas found.

55
00:02:44,473 --> 00:02:46,223
Step two, your first chart.

56
00:02:46,223 --> 00:02:48,685
Import matplotlib dot pyplot as p l t.

57
00:02:48,685 --> 00:02:50,889
That's another name everyone uses.

58
00:02:50,889 --> 00:02:52,185
Read the file again.

59
00:02:52,185 --> 00:02:54,000
Then one line does the work.

60
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,981
d f dot plot, x equals Month, y equals Coffee.

61
00:02:56,981 --> 00:02:59,962
The x is the horizontal axis, y is the values.

62
00:02:59,962 --> 00:03:05,666
Then plt dot savefig saves the picture to a file, and plt dot show opens it in a window.

63
00:03:06,366 --> 00:03:07,624
Run it, and here it is.

64
00:03:07,624 --> 00:03:09,156
Coffee sales month by month.

65
00:03:09,156 --> 00:03:13,313
You can see the dip in the summer, and the big climb at the end of the year.

66
00:03:13,313 --> 00:03:17,143
That's something you'd never notice by staring at a column of numbers.

67
00:03:17,143 --> 00:03:19,058
It works, but it looks a bit plain.

68
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:19,878
Let's fix that.

69
00:03:20,578 --> 00:03:21,226
Step three.

70
00:03:21,226 --> 00:03:22,346
Five small changes.

71
00:03:22,346 --> 00:03:28,476
First, give y a list of columns, Coffee, Tea and Cakes, and you get three lines, with a legend for free.

72
00:03:28,476 --> 00:03:30,892
Marker equals o puts a dot on each month.

73
00:03:30,892 --> 00:03:34,192
Figsize sets the size in inches, ten wide and five tall.

74
00:03:34,192 --> 00:03:36,078
Then set title, and set y label.

75
00:03:36,078 --> 00:03:39,084
Tight layout stops the labels from getting cut off.

76
00:03:39,084 --> 00:03:42,326
And d p i equals one fifty makes the saved image sharp.

77
00:03:43,026 --> 00:03:43,722
Run it again.

78
00:03:43,722 --> 00:03:46,026
Now that's a chart you can put in a report.

79
00:03:46,026 --> 00:03:49,402
Three products, a title, labeled axes, and dots on every month.

80
00:03:49,402 --> 00:03:51,010
And look at what it tells you.

81
00:03:51,010 --> 00:03:54,976
Tea sinks in the summer, cakes climb, and coffee takes off from September.

82
00:03:54,976 --> 00:04:00,549
Notice that matplotlib shows every other month on the bottom, so the labels don't crash into each other.

83
00:04:01,249 --> 00:04:03,009
So where did the pictures go?

84
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:05,193
Into the same folder as your script.

85
00:04:05,193 --> 00:04:12,899
List the folder, and there they are, line one dot png and line all dot png, ready to paste into an email, a slide, or a report.

86
00:04:12,899 --> 00:04:14,356
Want a P D F, for print?

87
00:04:14,356 --> 00:04:19,028
Just change the end of the file name to dot p d f, and savefig does the rest.

88
00:04:19,728 --> 00:04:21,084
Step four, a bar chart.

89
00:04:21,084 --> 00:04:23,502
First we need the total for each product.

90
00:04:23,502 --> 00:04:27,925
Pick the three columns, with two pairs of square brackets, and add dot sum.

91
00:04:27,925 --> 00:04:30,048
Pandas adds up every column for you.

92
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:32,289
Then totals dot plot, kind equals bar.

93
00:04:32,289 --> 00:04:34,529
Color takes a list, one color per bar.

94
00:04:34,529 --> 00:04:36,770
Rot equals zero keeps the labels flat.

95
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:40,131
And bar label writes the exact number on top of each bar.

96
00:04:40,832 --> 00:04:42,709
The print shows the real totals.

97
00:04:42,709 --> 00:04:45,641
Twenty six thousand one hundred thirty for coffee.

98
00:04:45,641 --> 00:04:49,220
Ninety two eighty for tea, and ninety three ninety for cakes.

99
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:51,038
And the chart makes it obvious.

100
00:04:51,038 --> 00:04:54,323
Coffee sells almost three times more than anything else.

101
00:04:55,023 --> 00:04:56,204
Here's the picture.

102
00:04:56,204 --> 00:05:01,425
Three bars, the exact number on top of each one, and colors that match the products.

103
00:05:01,425 --> 00:05:04,098
This is the chart your boss actually reads.

104
00:05:04,798 --> 00:05:06,299
Step five, a pie chart.

105
00:05:06,299 --> 00:05:08,453
Same totals, but kind equals pie.

106
00:05:08,453 --> 00:05:10,541
Auto p c t adds the percentages.

107
00:05:10,541 --> 00:05:18,307
That little code, percent one point zero f, percent percent, just means a number with no decimals, then a percent sign.

108
00:05:18,307 --> 00:05:22,940
And set y label with empty quotes removes a leftover label on the side.

109
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:26,183
Coffee is fifty eight percent of the year.

110
00:05:26,183 --> 00:05:28,544
Tea and cakes, twenty one percent each.

111
00:05:28,544 --> 00:05:30,482
One honest tip about pie charts.

112
00:05:30,482 --> 00:05:32,480
They only work with a few slices.

113
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,628
With more than five or six, use a bar chart instead.

114
00:05:35,628 --> 00:05:37,324
It's much easier to compare.

115
00:05:38,024 --> 00:05:41,119
Now, the three errors that stop almost everyone.

116
00:05:41,119 --> 00:05:41,764
Error one.

117
00:05:41,764 --> 00:05:44,988
You run the script and get Import openpyxl failed.

118
00:05:44,988 --> 00:05:47,825
That means the Excel helper isn't installed.

119
00:05:47,825 --> 00:05:49,954
The fix is in the message itself.

120
00:05:49,954 --> 00:05:53,307
Run pip install openpyxl, and run your script again.

121
00:05:54,007 --> 00:05:54,589
Error two.

122
00:05:54,589 --> 00:05:55,463
File not found.

123
00:05:55,463 --> 00:06:01,926
Python looks for the file in the folder your terminal is in, not in the folder where you saved it in your head.

124
00:06:01,926 --> 00:06:07,690
Use c d to move the terminal into the folder with the script and the spreadsheet, and run it again.

125
00:06:08,390 --> 00:06:09,133
Error three.

126
00:06:09,133 --> 00:06:10,248
Key error, coffee.

127
00:06:10,248 --> 00:06:11,053
Look closely.

128
00:06:11,053 --> 00:06:15,573
I typed coffee with a small c, but the column is Coffee with a capital C.

129
00:06:15,573 --> 00:06:18,545
Column names must match the spreadsheet exactly.

130
00:06:18,545 --> 00:06:20,774
Capital letters, spaces, everything.

131
00:06:20,774 --> 00:06:23,993
Print d f dot columns, and copy the name from there.

132
00:06:24,694 --> 00:06:27,180
And one more, that's not an error at all.

133
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:32,394
If you run the script and no window appears, check that plt dot show is the last line.

134
00:06:32,394 --> 00:06:35,365
And always call savefig before show, never after.

135
00:06:35,365 --> 00:06:39,913
Once the window closes, the figure is gone, and you'd save a blank picture.

136
00:06:40,613 --> 00:06:41,380
Quick bonus.

137
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:42,659
Want the best month?

138
00:06:42,659 --> 00:06:43,810
Make a new column.

139
00:06:43,810 --> 00:06:47,839
d f, Total, equals the three columns, dot sum, axis equals one.

140
00:06:47,839 --> 00:06:51,931
Axis one means add across each row, instead of down each column.

141
00:06:51,931 --> 00:06:55,128
Then idx max finds the row with the biggest total.

142
00:06:55,828 --> 00:06:56,251
Run it.

143
00:06:56,251 --> 00:07:01,082
Every month now has a total, and the best month is December, with fifty one ten.

144
00:07:01,082 --> 00:07:05,672
Two lines of code, and you'd never have to scroll and compare by hand again.

145
00:07:06,372 --> 00:07:08,390
Now, the dashboard from the start.

146
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:14,685
plt dot subplots, one comma two, creates one figure with two empty charts side by side, ax one and ax two.

147
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:16,347
Then the trick is ax equals.

148
00:07:16,347 --> 00:07:18,841
You tell each plot which space to draw in.

149
00:07:18,841 --> 00:07:20,742
The line chart goes into ax one.

150
00:07:20,742 --> 00:07:22,582
The bar chart goes into ax two.

151
00:07:22,582 --> 00:07:25,730
And one savefig saves the whole thing as one picture.

152
00:07:26,430 --> 00:07:28,525
Run it, and the dashboard is saved.

153
00:07:28,525 --> 00:07:31,758
Monthly sales on the left, yearly totals on the right.

154
00:07:31,758 --> 00:07:37,805
Next month, paste the new numbers into the spreadsheet, run the same command, and the report is done.

155
00:07:37,805 --> 00:07:39,302
That's the real shortcut.

156
00:07:40,002 --> 00:07:40,501
Pro tip.

157
00:07:40,501 --> 00:07:44,114
That word kind is all you change to try a different chart.

158
00:07:44,114 --> 00:07:47,479
Bar h gives you horizontal bars, great for long names.

159
00:07:47,479 --> 00:07:49,784
Area fills the space under the lines.

160
00:07:49,784 --> 00:07:51,591
Scatter compares two columns.

161
00:07:51,591 --> 00:07:53,896
Hist shows how values are spread out.

162
00:07:53,896 --> 00:07:56,950
Same data, same line of code, one word different.

163
00:07:57,650 --> 00:07:58,418
Quick recap.

164
00:07:58,418 --> 00:08:00,979
Install pandas, matplotlib and openpyxl.

165
00:08:00,979 --> 00:08:02,900
Read the file with read excel.

166
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,949
Draw with d f dot plot, x and y.

167
00:08:04,949 --> 00:08:06,422
Add a title and labels.

168
00:08:06,422 --> 00:08:09,496
Use sum for totals, and kind for the chart type.

169
00:08:09,496 --> 00:08:11,481
Save with savefig, before show.

170
00:08:11,481 --> 00:08:14,554
And subplots puts everything into one dashboard.

171
